df\\\": [], \\\"row_degree\\\": [], \\\"weighted_matrix\\\": []}\\n    n_rows = len(matrix)\\n    n_cols = len(matrix[0])\\n    df = [0] * n_cols\\n    for row in matrix:\\n        for j, val in enumerate(row):\\n            if val:\\n                df[j] += 1\\n    idf = [math.log(n_rows / df[j]) if df[j] > 0 else 0.0 for j in range(n_cols)]\\n    row_degree = [sum(row) for row in matrix]\\n    weighted_matrix = [[idf[j] if matrix[i][j] else 0.0 for j in range(n_cols)] for i in range(n_rows)]\\n    return {\\\"idf\\\": idf, \\\"row_degree\\\": row_degree, \\\"weighted_matrix\\\": weighted_matrix}\"\n\nBut note: In Python, the use of 'matrix' variable: case is the payload, which has key 'matrix'. That's fine.\n\nWe need to be careful with string quotes inside: The code uses single quotes for 'matrix', but we can keep them. The JSON string will use double quotes for the whole value, so single quotes inside are fine.\n\nWe must escape any double quotes inside: we have return dictionaries with keys in double quotes: {\"idf\": ...}. So we need to escape those as \\\". So in the string: return {\\\"idf\\\": idf, \\\"row_degree\\\": row_degree, \\\"weighted_matrix\\\": weighted_matrix}. Also the early return line similarly.\n\nAlso check: the condition if not matrix: but matrix might be empty list. But we also need to handle case where matrix has rows but maybe some rows empty? Not needed.\n\nLet's produce final JSON. Ensure no extra spaces. The envelope: \"Your entire response must be exactly one valid JSON object with the single key \"implementation\".\" So output just that JSON.\n\nI'll output:\n\n{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    import math\\n    matrix = case['matrix']\\n    if not matrix:\\n        return {\\\"idf\\\": [], \\\"row_degree\\\": [], \\\"weighted_matrix\\\": []}\\n    n_rows = len(matrix)\\n    n_cols = len(matrix[0])\\n    df = [0] * n_cols\\n    for row in matrix:\\n        for j, val in enumerate(row):\\n            if val:\\n           